 perfect is not the standard past-regret form after wish.","B":"Had needs a past participle here, not -ing.","C":"Would + base normally asks for changed behaviour rather than rewriting a finished past event.","D":"A regret about yesterday uses **had + prepared** after wish."}}},{"id":"2c85905f-2711-5af0-a6b6-c98aede5c314","topicSlug":"wishes","level":"B2","prompt":"You want someone to change an annoying habit: “I wish they would ____ the evening meal without being asked.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"to prepare"},{"id":"B","text":"prepare"},{"id":"C","text":"prepares"},{"id":"D","text":"prepared"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The change is expressed by **would + base verb**.","explanation":"After **would**, keep base **prepare** for the desired change in behaviour.","keywords":["wish-behaviour"],"skill":"wish-behaviour","contextKey":"wishes-49","fingerprint":"78ee84fe6a6f96d2b10f6bb4458518b74eb69721e3e4645204dd3c374c62b084","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-wishes"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The change is expressed by **would + base verb**.","After **would**, keep base **prepare** for the desired change in behaviour.","Choose **prepare**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Would takes a bare infinitive without to.","B":"After **would**, keep base **prepare** for the desired change in behaviour.","C":"Would takes a base form without -s.","D":"A past form cannot directly follow would."}}},{"id":"bb113d26-e05c-55f5-a7f6-9027cdc6c452","topicSlug":"wishes","level":"B2","prompt":"You lack the ability: “If only I could ____ the evening meal by myself.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"to prepare"},{"id":"B","text":"prepare"},{"id":"C","text":"preparing"},{"id":"D","text":"prepares"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Could already marks the desired ability; use the **base verb** after it.","explanation":"**Could + prepare** expresses the wished-for ability.","keywords":["wish-ability"],"skill":"wish-ability","contextKey":"wishes-49","fingerprint":"2b7928fe3fb899cb2074555dcbeb4d65a40edd410f8e3c2688ee51477eb7fade","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-wishes"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Could already marks the desired ability; use the **base verb** after it.","**Could + prepare** expresses the wished-for ability.","Choose **prepare**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Could takes a bare infinitive.","B":"**Could + prepare** expresses the wished-for ability.","C":"Could alone does not take -ing.","D":"Could does not take third-person -s on the next verb."}}},{"id":"b64ee7bb-6caa-55ef-a481-22deafe84476","topicSlug":"wishes","level":"B2","prompt":"Use a past form for an unreal present wish: “I wish she ____ the rowing boat more often.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repairing"},{"id":"B","text":"to repair"},{"id":"C","text":"repaired"},{"id":"D","text":"repairs"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"This is an **unreal present wish**; past form marks distance from reality.","explanation":"The wish imagines a different present habit, so it uses past-form **repaired**.","keywords":["present-wish"],"skill":"present-wish","contextKey":"wishes-50","fingerprint":"129b7ea2f02fa3167c9a76fbfcfb2a16b85233092c37c2824af952984f32aae3","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-wishes"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["This is an **unreal present wish**; past form marks distance from reality.","The wish imagines a different present habit, so it uses past-form **repaired**.","Choose **repaired**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"An -ing form alone cannot complete the finite clause.","B":"A to-infinitive does not supply the finite predicate after she.","C":"The wish imagines a different present habit, so it uses past-form **repaired**.","D":"The ordinary unreal-present wish uses a past form rather than present -s."}}},{"id":"21c05668-549c-5e15-a400-27a823e515e0","topicSlug":"wishes","level":"B2","prompt":"The task was not done yesterday: “I wish I ____ the rowing boat then.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"had repaired"},{"id":"B","text":"have repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"had repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"would repair"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"The time **then** refers to a finished past; use **past perfect** for the regret.","explanation":"A regret about yesterday uses **had + repaired** after wish.","keywords":["past-regret"],"skill":"past-regret","contextKey":"wishes-50","fingerprint":"0abd6efda5bc9882c769be5eacb95232b483a25dbdbf61db19b6bb9d87644833","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-wishes"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The time **then** refers to a finished past; use **past perfect** for the regret.","A regret about yesterday uses **had + repaired** after wish.","Choose **had repaired**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A regret about yesterday uses **had + repaired** after wish.","B":"Present perfect is not the standard past-regret form after wish.","C":"Had needs a past participle here, not -ing.","D":"Would + base normally asks for changed behaviour rather than rewriting a finished past event."}}},{"id":"891a46df-5561-5c7a-a89c-01d3bc9aab71","topicSlug":"wishes","level":"B2","prompt":"You want someone to change an annoying habit: “I wish they would ____ the rowing boat without being asked.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"to repair"},{"id":"B","text":"repair"},{"id":"C","text":"repairs"},{"id":"D","text":"repaired"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The change is expressed by **would + base verb**.","explanation":"After **would**, keep base **repair** for the desired change in behaviour.","keywords":["wish-behaviour"],"skill":"wish-behaviour","contextKey":"wishes-50","fingerprint":"9304c5a3d2c7ad0963dfffcb64dfb8c1645dde9d41fdb3c94f577d35e322c1bc","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-wishes"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The change is expressed by **would + base verb**.","After **would**, keep base **repair** for the desired change in behaviour.","Choose **repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Would takes a bare infinitive without to.","B":"After **would**, keep base **repair** for the desired change in behaviour.","C":"Would takes a base form without -s.","D":"A past form cannot directly follow would."}}},{"id":"63e1077f-9542-5a0a-a7b9-ee219f0abd4f","topicSlug":"wishes","level":"B2","prompt":"You lack the ability: “If only I could ____ the rowing boat by myself.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repair"},{"id":"B","text":"repairing"},{"id":"C","text":"repairs"},{"id":"D","text":"to repair"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Could already marks the desired ability; use the **base verb** after it.","explanation":"**Could + repair** expresses the wished-for ability.","keywords":["wish-ability"],"skill":"wish-ability","contextKey":"wishes-50","fingerprint":"115101e4e21db917e7601f33eaffbb130a49f7e3c1ac49048c1c6c203ba2ffd8","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-wishes"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Could already marks the desired ability; use the **base verb** after it.","**Could + repair** expresses the wished-for ability.","Choose **repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**Could + repair** expresses the wished-for ability.","B":"Could alone does not take -ing.","C":"Could does not take third-person -s on the next verb.","D":"Could takes a bare infinitive."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Express a desired change in the present, regret about the past or dissatisfaction with someone’s behaviour.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"An unreal present wish","explanation":"After wish or if only, use a past form to imagine a present situation differently. This is sometimes called unreal past: its form is past, but its reference can be now. With be, were is common for all persons in formal hypothetical uses.","pattern":"I wish I **knew** the answer. / If only I **were** taller.","commonMistakes":["A future hope that may come true normally uses hope, not wish + will in the same pattern."],"examples":["Use a past form for an unreal present wish: “I wish she **prepared** the visitor guide more often.”","Use a past form for an unreal present wish: “I wish she **restored** the old portrait more often.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Regret about the past","explanation":"Use **wish + past perfect** to regret an earlier event or non-event. The past perfect marks the fact that the past cannot now be changed. The wish describes an alternative to it.","pattern":"I wish I **had checked** the time.","commonMistakes":["I wish I checked yesterday does not give the standard past-regret form; use had checked."],"examples":["The task was not done yesterday: “I wish I **had prepared** the visitor guide then.”","The task was not done yesterday: “I wish I **had restored** the old portrait then.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"A wish for changed behaviour","explanation":"Wish + would often expresses a desire for another person or an external situation to change, especially an irritating repeated behaviour. It normally concerns willingness or behaviour, rather than a simple characteristic.","pattern":"I wish they **would listen**.","commonMistakes":["I wish I would be taller is not the normal way to wish for a different characteristic."],"examples":["You want someone to change an annoying habit: “I wish they would **prepare** the visitor guide without being asked.”","You want someone to change an annoying habit: “I wish they would **restore** the old portrait without being asked.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Ability and wishes","explanation":"Wish + could can express a desired ability or opportunity. Compare I wish I could come with I hope I can come: the former suggests a barrier, while the latter leaves the possibility open. If only usually makes the wish more emphatic.","pattern":"If only I **could stay** longer.","commonMistakes":["Choose wish or hope from the speaker’s view of reality, not just from whether the sentence refers to the future."],"examples":["You lack the ability: “If only I could **prepare** the visitor guide by myself.”","You lack the ability: “If only I could **restore** the old portrait by myself.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["I wish I **knew** the answer. / If only I **were** taller.","I wish I **had checked** the time.","I wish they **would listen**.","If only I **could stay** longer."]},"stage":"4 · Connecting meanings","prerequisites":["third-mixed-conditionals"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
